# Line endings.
#
# This repository is written on Windows and runs on Linux, and for most files that difference is
# invisible — git normalises to LF on commit and the tooling on both sides copes.
#
# Two kinds of file do NOT cope, and both of them are in the deploy path:
#
#   * A YAML block scalar (the `- |` shell script inside deploy/compose/prod.yml's backup service)
#     is only valid with LF line breaks. Checked out with CRLF, the file stops being parseable
#     YAML — and the first anybody would know of it is a production deploy failing at the moment
#     the stack is replaced.
#
#   * A shell script with CRLF fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", which is a error
#     message that says nothing about line endings to anybody who has not met it before.
#
# So these are pinned to LF in the working tree as well as in the object store. Everything else
# keeps git's normal behaviour.

*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
